# Resolution
Fixed in `7cde011 Preserve matching multipart completion retry outcome`.

The multipart completion publisher no longer uses the generic snapshot-sensitive
install-or-drain wrapper at the final object-PG command install point. If
another command wins that pending slot, completion now restarts the retry loop
without draining it. That allows the existing matching-pending
`CommitMultipartObject` branch to observe an equivalent completion command,
finish that exact command, and return the command-derived success outcome
instead of draining it generically and later returning false `NoSuchUpload`.

The fix adds
`multipart_completion_pending_install_conflict_with_matching_completion_returns_success`,
which deterministically inserts an equivalent pending completion after the
top-of-loop pending check and before install. The regression asserts the caller
receives the successful completion outcome and the command stream is clean.

Verified before the fix commit:
- `cargo fmt`
- `./scripts/check-storage-cluster-boundaries`
- `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`
- `cargo nextest run -p storage multipart_completion --no-fail-fast`
- `cargo nextest run`

# Summary
Introduced a non-security correctness/availability regression in multipart completion contention handling. It does not appear to enable unauthorized access, data disclosure, or code execution, but it can make valid concurrent/retried multipart completion requests fail spuriously after the object has been committed.
In complete_multipart_upload_commit_serialized, the new install_snapshot_sensitive_metadata_command_or_drain call drains a winning pending command before returning ContenderDrained. That is unsafe for this path because the loop has special handling for a pending CommitMultipartObject that matches the current request: it computes the completion outcome, applies the command, prunes retention state, and returns success. If another coordinator installs a matching completion command after the top-of-loop pending check but before this request installs its own command, the new helper drains and applies that matching command generically, discards the outcome, and then restarts. On restart, the pending command is gone and the upload is no longer InProgress, so the request can return NoSuchUpload even though the object was successfully committed. The previous code continued without draining at this point, allowing the next loop iteration to observe the pending matching command and return the correct outcome.

# Validation
## Rubric
- [x] Confirm the normal matching-pending multipart completion path applies the command and returns the computed success outcome.
- [x] Confirm the new install-or-drain helper drains/applies a contender command before returning `ContenderDrained`.
- [x] Deterministically reproduce the timing window where a matching `CommitMultipartObject` appears after the top-of-loop pending check but before object command install.
- [x] Show the caller receives `NoSuchUpload` after the matching completion was drained/applied, instead of the successful completion outcome.
- [x] Attempt dynamic validation beyond code reading: direct failing test/backtrace, valgrind attempt, and LLDB debugger trace.
## Report
Validated the suspected availability/correctness regression. I first confirmed the relevant control flow: `complete_multipart_upload_commit_serialized` has special matching-pending completion handling at `crates/storage/src/cluster/request_ops.rs:5707-5723`, but after a retry it returns `NoSuchUpload` if the upload is no longer `InProgress` at `request_ops.rs:5729-5738`. The new contention helper is called at `request_ops.rs:5915-5920`; on conflict it invokes `drain_pending_object_metadata_commands_for_bucket` at `crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:1042-1046`, whose drain loop applies pending commands at `cluster.rs:1842-1855` without returning the completion outcome to this caller.

As a positive control, the existing test `multipart_completion_drains_matching_pending_completion` passed, confirming that if the matching pending command exists before the top-of-loop check, the special path succeeds.

I then added a targeted unit test using the existing `test_install_before_metadata_command_pending_install_hook`. The test constructs a valid multipart completion, precomputes an equivalent winning `CommitMultipartObject`, and inserts it exactly when the current request is about to install its object-PG command. This deterministically models the suspected race: after the top-of-loop pending check but before this request installs its own command. Running `RUST_BACKTRACE=1 cargo test -p storage multipart_completion_pending_install_conflict_with_matching_completion_returns_success -- --nocapture` produced:

`inserted matching pending completion during object command install`

then panicked because `complete_multipart_upload_commit_serialized` returned:

`Metadata(NoSuchUpload { upload_id: "racecomplete..." })`

instead of the expected successful completion outcome. This matches the finding: the winning matching command was drained/applied generically, then the loop retried and saw the upload was no longer `InProgress`, causing `NoSuchUpload`.

Valgrind was attempted but unavailable in the container (`bash: command not found: valgrind`). I then used LLDB non-interactively with a breakpoint on `core::panicking::panic_fmt`; it stopped at the test panic and showed the stack through `Result::expect` at `crates/storage/src/cluster/local.rs:18777`, confirming the observed erroneous `NoSuchUpload` result from the targeted repro.

